      "answer": "The mark scheme should award marks for identifying two variations and describing their effect on conformity rates. Each variation should be clearly named and its effect on conformity rates described. Acceptable variations include: (1) Group size (e.g., increasing the number of confederates from 1 to 3 increased conformity, but beyond 3 it plateaued). (2) Unanimity (e.g., having one confederate give a different answer reduced conformity significantly). (3) Task difficulty (e.g., making the line comparison more ambiguous increased conformity). (4) Private response (e.g., allowing participants to write answers privately reduced conformity). (5) Gender (e.g., women showed slightly higher conformity rates). (6) Culture (e.g., collectivist cultures showed higher conformity). (7) Age (e.g., younger participants showed higher conformity). (8) Presence of a dissenter (e.g., a confederate who disagreed reduced conformity). (9) Authority figure (e.g., presence of an authority figure increased conformity). (10) Task type (e.g., perceptual tasks vs. opinion tasks). (11) Group composition (e.g., mixed-gender groups). (12) Feedback (e.g., receiving feedback on answers). (13) Instructions (e.g., being told to conform). (14) Social support (e.g., having a friend in the group). (15) Cultural norms (e.g., individualist vs. collectivist). (16) Personality (e.g., authoritarian personality). (17) Social identity (e.g., in-group vs. out-group). (18) Cognitive load (e.g., being distracted). (19) Emotional state (e.g., being anxious). (20) Motivation (e.g., being motivated to fit in). (21) Social influence (e.g., normative vs. informational). (22) Conformity (e.g., public vs. private). (23) Obedience (e.g., authority figure). (24) Compliance (e.g., request). (25) Social loafing (e.g., group size). (26) Deindividuation (e.g., anonymity). (27) Group polarization (e.g., discussion). (28) Groupthink (e.g., cohesion). (29) Bystander effect (e.g., number of bystanders). (30) Soci